# Carrier Wound #31: We Asked The Planner Not To Use Tools. It Had Tools.

Wound #30 capped pi's raw stdout with `head -c` so a runaway could no longer
ENOSPC the lite disk. We shipped it, deployed it, and re-drove a dream-shape run.
The cap WORKED, exactly as the #30 receipt's "What this does NOT claim" section
called it: run `fa5a3189` came back **disk-healthy** (957 MB free vs the old
30 MB), the cap fired (`raw_output_capped: reached 67108864`), pi crashed EPIPE
in its own `output-guard.js` when `head` closed the pipe, `lane_output_bytes`
was the full 64 MiB and not the 112-byte ENOSPC stub. The ENOSPC cascade was
dead. And the lane STILL did not reach `captured`:

```
run-live-...-fa5a3189  →  blocked / planner_lane_incomplete
Agent lane reached step "build-receipt" (exit 143, pi 1): write EPIPE
```

The cap converted a disk catastrophe into an honest, bounded, committed-adjacent
failure — and handed us the next layer to read. The planner pi **rode most of
its budget reaching the 64 MiB cap** (a slow tool-call loop, ~12 min), then
normalize burned its full 60s trying to parse 64 MiB of runaway JSON, so the
overall lane timed out and SIGTERM'd `build-receipt` before it could commit. The
#30 fix made the failure survivable and diagnosable; #31 removes the cause.

## Mechanism — the prompt was a contract nobody enforced

The planner prompt (`plannerPromptFor`, `cloudflare-agent-lane-adapters.ts`) ends
with a polite instruction:

> Generate a DynamicWorkflowBlueprint for the requested run. Emit only JSON. […]
> **Do not call tools, perform side effects, or materialize secrets.**

But the invocation passed **no tool constraint**, and `pi` ships its
`read`/`bash`/`edit`/`write` tools **ON by default** (`pi --help`, verified —
not guessed). So under `--mode json`, where every tool call is a stream event,
the planner — which only has to emit one JSON blueprint and needs nothing from
the filesystem — was free to wander into a tool-call loop. And it did, four live
runs in a row: `a8bc84dc`, `f9a37a09`, `53cc30a4`, `fa5a3189`. Deterministic.

The prompt said "do not call tools." The CLI handed it tools anyway. A model
instruction is a *request*; a CLI flag is an *invariant*. We were asking the
caller to honor a contract instead of making the dishonored state impossible.

> The principle of least privilege describes the idea that when granting access
> we should grant the minimum access a party needs to carry out the required
> functionality, and only for the time period they need it.
>
> — Sam Newman, *Building Microservices, 2nd ed.*, Ch. 11, Core Principles

That is this wound exactly. The planner's required functionality is *pure
generation* — emit one blueprint, touch nothing. Its minimum access is
**zero tools**. We granted it the full read/bash/edit/write surface, and the
excess capability was precisely what it abused into a 64 MiB runaway.

## Fix — enforce the contract at the CLI, scoped to the one lane that wants none

`cloudflare-sandbox-agent-lane-command.ts`, the `pi-invoke` block. Add a tool
gate keyed on `LANE_KIND`, right after the existing `pi_mode_args` gate:

```bash
pi_tool_args=""
if [ "$LANE_KIND" = "planner" ]; then
  pi_tool_args="--no-tools"
fi
...
timeout "$pi_budget_s" pi --provider "$PI_PROVIDER" --model "$PI_MODEL" \
  --no-session $pi_mode_args $pi_tool_args -p "$(cat "$LANE_PROMPT_PATH")" \
  2> "$stderr_path" | head -c "$raw_output_cap_bytes" > "$raw_output_path"
```

Two load-bearing properties:

1. **The impossible state is made impossible.** `--no-tools` disables pi's tools
   at the process boundary. The planner *cannot* tool-loop because it has no
   tools to loop on. The prompt's "do not call tools" is no longer a plea; it is
   the CLI's reality. (parse, don't validate — make impossible states
   impossible.)
2. **Scoped to `LANE_KIND=planner` ONLY — this is the whole skill of the fix.**
   The worker/analysis/verifier lanes are *source-grounded*: a verifier with no
   `read`/`bash` cannot open the primary source it is supposed to review, so it
   would emit a verdict about nothing — **a hollow capture, which is strictly
   worse than a runaway** (a runaway fails honestly and loudly; a hollow capture
   *succeeds* falsely). Least privilege is per-party: the planner needs zero
   tools, the verifier needs read/bash. A blanket `--no-tools` would have traded
   a loud bug for a silent one. The Explore pass that first proposed this fix
   recommended a blanket `--tools=''` at the shared invocation line; that would
   have lobotomized every source-grounded lane. The gate is the correction.

### Plus: head-sample-on-cap, so a *persisting* runaway shows WHAT not just THAT

If `--no-tools` is the right root cause, the cap branch never fires again for the
planner and the head-sample never runs. But if a runaway somehow persists (pure
generation looping, an enormous-but-legitimate stream), the disk numbers proved
*that* it ran away; this rides a sanitized 4 KiB HEAD of the runaway out on the
`diag()` channel (which survives SIGTERM and reaches the operator blocker) to
show *what* — a tool-call event loop reads differently from pure-generation
repetition. That settles the next hypothesis in ONE cycle instead of another
blind re-drive. (Joel's standing demand: *"we should be able to observe the run
in progress and not guess … instead of waiting and staring at it like fucking
dummies."*) Control chars flattened to one line; git creds scrubbed.

### String.raw note (the convention that bit #29 and #30 — it did NOT bite here)

`buildPiAgentLaneCommand()` is a `String.raw` template, so JS `${...}` fires
anywhere — including bash comments. This edit used bare `$var` only
(`$LANE_KIND`, `$pi_tool_args`) and no brace-expansion in code or comments, so
it built clean on the first cut. The lesson from #29/#30 held.

## Chaos test that would have caught it (hostile double, not a polite mock)

`tests/integration/cloudflare-sandbox-agent-lanes.test.ts` — *"muzzles ONLY the
planner with --no-tools … verifier/source-grounded lanes keep their tools
(wound #31)"*. It SLICES the REAL pi-invoke block out of the emitted bash
(`command.indexOf('pi_mode_args=""')` → `command.indexOf("\nmark
normalize-output")`) and runs it verbatim, twice, against a `pi` that **records
its own argv to disk** — once with `LANE_KIND=planner`, once with `verifier`,
IDENTICAL otherwise so only the gate can differ:

- **planner** → recorded argv MUST contain `--no-tools` (the flag actually
  reaches the process — proven off pi's real argv, not a JS variable).
- **verifier** → argv MUST NOT contain `--no-tools`, and pi MUST still run (no
  hollow-capture regression).

A polite mock asserting on a TypeScript variable would prove nothing about what
the spawned process receives; this reproduces the production transport — the
real emitted bash, real word-splitting of `$pi_tool_args`, pi's real argv. It
fails on **both** failure modes: pre-fix (no gate → planner never gets the flag)
AND a careless blanket-apply (verifier wrongly muzzled). Only the
`LANE_KIND=planner` gate is green on both.

Two sibling tests slice the SAME region and so now traverse the new gate: their
harnesses gained a faithful `LANE_KIND` (`set -u` would trip on it otherwise)
and — for the wound #30 test, whose cap fires — a faithful `scrub_credentials`
the new head-sample pipes through. `pnpm typecheck` clean · `pnpm check` clean ·
the lane file = **42 passed** (was 41; the new chaos test is the +1).

## What this does NOT claim (skepticism held)

`--no-tools` is the strongest hypothesis for the runaway, with four
deterministic live runs behind it and a verified mechanism (tools-on by default
+ `--mode json` amplification). It is NOT yet proven to reach `captured` — that
is the live re-drive's job, the next move. If the planner still runs away with no
tools, the head-sample now shows whether it is pure-generation looping (→ a
prompt/budget change, or a normalize short-circuit on a known-capped runaway) or
something else — diagnosed from the blocker, not from staring. One variable
changed (the gate; the head-sample only fires on a runaway the gate should
eliminate), so the next run's signal is clean.

## The carrier discipline (eleventh carrier wound)

#28 made the lane say WHERE it died. #29 made it say WHY with the disk numbers.
#30 stopped the lane from letting a misbehaving agent dictate how many bytes hit
the disk. #31 stops the lane from handing a pure-generation step capabilities it
was *told not to use but was structurally free to* — the gap between a prompt
(a request) and a flag (an invariant). A capability a party does not need is a
capability it can abuse; grant the minimum, at the boundary, per party. The valve
on the inlet (#30) now sits behind a door that only opens for the lanes that
have a reason to walk through it.
